    <h3 id="Introduction">Introduction</h3>
    <p>
        There are different types of hydrogen, the main ones being Green, Grey, and Blue, we will be mainly focusing on Green. 
        It is produced by electrolysis from renewable sources, which involves breaking down water molecules (H2O) into oxygen (O2) 
        and hydrogen (H2) <a href="https://www.iberdrola.com/sustainability/green-hydrogen" target="_blank">(Iberdrola, 2022)</a>. 
        This is the general overview, but to delve further, there are three main steps to obtain Hydrogen: Molecular Transformation, 
        Gasification, and Water Electrolysis. In the initial stages, a high temperature water vapor is used to separate the carbon from 
        the hydrogen that makes up the natural gas <a href="www.acciona.com" target="_blank">(www.acciona.com, n.d.)</a> to obtain dihydrogen and carbon dioxide. 
        Then in Gasification, the biomass is burned in a reactor at high temperatures <a href="https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuelcells/hydrogen-production-biomass-gasification" target="_blank">(US Department of Energy, 2019)</a> 
        to make a desired yield. Finally, the products undergo water electrolysis to separate the hydrogen completely with the help 
        of direct electrical current which connects to the anode and cathode via the water.
    </p>

