    <h3 id="Analysis_and_discussion">Analysis and Discussion</h3>

    <h4>How do we produce Hydrogen?</h4>
    <p>
        There are different types of hydrogen, the main ones being Green, Grey, and Blue. We will be mainly focusing on Green. It is produced
        by electrolysis from renewable sources, which involves breaking down water molecules (H2O) into oxygen (O2) and hydrogen (H2) 
        <a href="https://www.iberdrola.com/sustainability/green-hydrogen" target="_blank">(Iberdrola, 2022)</a>. This is the general overview, but to delve further, 
        there are three main steps: Molecular Transformation, Gasification, and Water Electrolysis. In the initial stages, high temperature water vapor 
        is used to separate the carbon from the hydrogen in natural gas to obtain dihydrogen and carbon dioxide. In Gasification, biomass is burned in a 
        reactor at high temperatures <a href="https://www.energy.gov/eere/fuelcells/hydrogen-production-biomass-gasification" target="_blank">(US Department of Energy, 2019)</a>. 
        Finally, the products undergo water electrolysis to separate the hydrogen completely with the help of direct electrical current.
    </p>

    <h4>How is Green Hydrogen Produced?</h4>
    <p>
        Green hydrogen is produced via electrolysis, where water molecules are split into hydrogen (H2) and oxygen (O2). Platinum electrodes are 
        used in the electrolyser because platinum is chemically inert and will not produce side products during the process. An electrolyte like sulfuric 
        acid is used as pure water will not carry a high enough charge density <a href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Introductory_Chemistry/Introductory_Chemistry_(CK-12)/23%3A_Electrochemistry/23.09%3A_Electrolysis_of_Water" target="_blank">(LibreTexts, 2016)</a>. 
        The yield of hydrogen produced from electrolysis is usually about 70-80% of the energy used.
    </p>

