    <h4>Pros of Hydrogen as a Renewable Energy Source</h4>
    <p>
        The pros outweigh the cons when it comes to using hydrogen to combat climate change. Green hydrogen does not produce any polluting gases, making it 
        invaluable as a renewable energy source <a href="https://www.iberdrola.com/sustainability/green-hydrogen" target="_blank">(Iberdrola, 2022)</a>. It also has a range of applications, 
        including use in the automotive industry to replace fossil fuels and reduce nitrogen oxide emissions <a href="https://www.qld.gov.au/environment/management/monitoring/air/air-pollution/pollutants/nitrogen-oxides" target="_blank">(Queensland Government, 2013)</a>.
    </p>

    <h4>Compromises of Using Hydrogen as a Renewable Energy Source</h4>
    <p>
        The primary issue with hydrogen production is cost. Processes like electrolysis require substantial energy, which is costly. Additionally, hydrogen is highly flammable and volatile, requiring extra safety measures during storage and transport.
    </p>

    <h4>Conclusion</h4>
    <p>
        Overall, investing in renewable energy is essential as global temperatures continue to rise. Green hydrogen, being a low-emission energy source, 
        is a promising candidate to help mitigate the impacts of climate change <a href="https://climate.copernicus.eu/year-2024-set-end-warmest-record" target="_blank">(Copernicus.eu, 2024)</a>.
    </p>

